00:04:26.850 --> 00:04:29.376
it's these people--

00:04:29.376 --> 00:04:32.310
not these specific three people,
because we have slightly more

00:04:32.310 --> 00:04:33.835
than that running--

00:04:33.835 --> 00:04:35.610
people working on
Google Cloud today--

00:04:35.610 --> 00:04:38.700
but Google Cloud
software engineers

00:04:38.700 --> 00:04:39.780
is what I meant to say.

00:04:39.780 --> 00:04:42.242
And these people
are the people who

00:04:42.242 --> 00:04:44.700
are responsible for making sure
that your service is always

00:04:44.700 --> 00:04:45.270
reliable.

00:04:45.270 --> 00:04:48.270
It's always up and
running when you need it.

00:04:48.270 --> 00:04:51.240
And part of working with a new
group of software engineers

00:04:51.240 --> 00:04:54.050
is developing a new
relationship of trust.

00:04:54.050 --> 00:04:57.000
So in your on-prem environment,
you probably had vendors.

00:04:57.000 --> 00:04:57.840
You had employees.

00:04:57.840 --> 00:04:59.820
You had people who were
working to make sure

00:04:59.820 --> 00:05:01.592
that your systems were reliable.

00:05:01.592 --> 00:05:03.300
And you developed a
relationship of trust

00:05:03.300 --> 00:05:05.910
with these people over
a long period of time.

00:05:05.910 --> 00:05:08.020
Moving to the public
cloud is no different.

00:05:08.020 --> 00:05:10.530
It just involves you entering
into a new relationship

00:05:10.530 --> 00:05:12.134
of trust with a new
group of people.

00:05:12.134 --> 00:05:14.550
And that's what we hope to
give you a bit more information

00:05:14.550 --> 00:05:15.466
about in today's talk.

00:05:18.630 --> 00:05:20.774
So far, I've spoken
about cloud migration.

00:05:20.774 --> 00:05:22.190
I've talked about
how important it

00:05:22.190 --> 00:05:24.430
is to changing the way
people do business--

00:05:24.430 --> 00:05:26.450
the flexibility,
the reliability,

00:05:26.450 --> 00:05:28.360
the cost management.

00:05:28.360 --> 00:05:30.650
And I've spoken about big
data and machine learning,

00:05:30.650 --> 00:05:32.630
how exciting that is, and
how many opportunities that's

00:05:32.630 --> 00:05:34.630
going to open up for all
of us in every business

00:05:34.630 --> 00:05:36.375
in every industry.

00:05:36.375 --> 00:05:38.750
And I've also started to talk
a little bit about security

00:05:38.750 --> 00:05:41.040
compliance and trust.

00:05:41.040 --> 00:05:45.170
Now, the challenge for you today
is that these three big themes

00:05:45.170 --> 00:05:47.390
can't be tackled independently.

00:05:47.390 --> 00:05:49.430
Every business is trying
to do all these things

00:05:49.430 --> 00:05:50.490
at the same time.

00:05:50.490 --> 00:05:53.294
And that's very, very difficult
in a changing environment.

00:05:53.294 --> 00:05:55.460
And it really, really helps
to have a cloud provider

00:05:55.460 --> 00:05:57.980
that you can trust, that
they're investing in security

00:05:57.980 --> 00:06:01.790
and compliance to bring you up
to speed at the rate you need.

00:06:01.790 --> 00:06:05.504
And that's going to be the
focus of today's presentation.

00:06:05.504 --> 00:06:08.350
So before we can enjoy all
these incredible benefits

00:06:08.350 --> 00:06:12.730
of being on the cloud, we need
to get a few things right.

00:06:12.730 --> 00:06:16.930
Security-- "Am I
secure in the cloud?"

00:06:16.930 --> 00:06:20.110
A recent survey by
HyTrust of cloud buyers

00:06:20.110 --> 00:06:24.580
globally found that the top-two
fears of people purchasing

00:06:24.580 --> 00:06:27.310
cloud were malicious
or accidental exposure

00:06:27.310 --> 00:06:30.460
of workload data and
uncontrolled or unmonitored

00:06:30.460 --> 00:06:31.741
access by admins.

00:06:31.741 --> 00:06:33.490
I want you to think
about those two things

00:06:33.490 --> 00:06:35.823
because we're going to come
back and touch on them later

00:06:35.823 --> 00:06:37.480
in this presentation.

00:06:37.480 --> 00:06:41.490
Compliance-- "Can I meet
my compliance obligations?"

00:06:41.490 --> 00:06:44.050
Now, this is not
just about compliance

00:06:44.050 --> 00:06:45.610
with external
policies that you may

00:06:45.610 --> 00:06:48.850
have to comply with or
external certifications.

00:06:48.850 --> 00:06:52.150
It's also about compliance
with your own internal policies

00:06:52.150 --> 00:06:54.130
and being able to have
visibility and certainty

00:06:54.130 --> 00:06:57.740
that you can certify that those
things have been abided by.

00:06:57.740 --> 00:07:00.190
And finally, trust--

00:07:00.190 --> 00:07:02.950
"Can I trust my cloud provider?"

00:07:02.950 --> 00:07:05.590
This is related to
security and compliance,

00:07:05.590 --> 00:07:07.120
but it's slightly different.

00:07:07.120 --> 00:07:09.182
This is more of an
emotional thing.

00:07:09.182 --> 00:07:11.390
Is the person that I'm in
business with along with me

00:07:11.390 --> 00:07:15.450
for the journey to
make me successful?

00:07:15.450 --> 00:07:17.700
So these are the three
needs that we need in order

00:07:17.700 --> 00:07:19.162
to move onto the cloud.

00:07:19.162 --> 00:07:21.210
But the way that
we see it at Google

00:07:21.210 --> 00:07:24.630
is that, in order to
meet these three needs,

00:07:24.630 --> 00:07:28.670
we need three foundational
pillars in place--

00:07:28.670 --> 00:07:34.020
technology, operations,
and accountability.

00:07:34.020 --> 00:07:37.810
And to talk to you more
about these three pillars,

00:07:37.810 --> 00:07:40.400
I'm going to invite Umesh.

00:07:40.400 --> 00:07:43.090
UMESH SHANKAR: Thanks, Joe.

00:07:43.090 --> 00:07:45.820
First thing we're going to
talk about is technology.

00:07:45.820 --> 00:07:48.580
If you don't have a
secure technology stack

00:07:48.580 --> 00:07:51.400
from the ground up, nothing
else really matters.

00:07:51.400 --> 00:07:54.520
No amount of operations,
no amount of accountability

00:07:54.520 --> 00:07:59.630
is going to save you if your
data is not fundamentally safe.

00:07:59.630 --> 00:08:03.320
We look at this problem as
a series of layers, right?

00:08:03.320 --> 00:08:05.325
You have your hardware
at the bottom.

00:08:05.325 --> 00:08:07.700
You have it all the way up
through your application layer

00:08:07.700 --> 00:08:09.408
at the top-- the
software that's actually

00:08:09.408 --> 00:08:11.270
running on the platform.

00:08:11.270 --> 00:08:14.000
And we've actually invested
in every one of these layers

00:08:14.000 --> 00:08:18.140
to make it secure, to harden it,
and to address all those three

00:08:18.140 --> 00:08:21.645
aspects that Joe talked about.

00:08:21.645 --> 00:08:23.370
So let's start at the bottom.

00:08:23.370 --> 00:08:27.070
We're going to dive
into each one in turn.

00:08:27.070 --> 00:08:31.120
It used to be the case,
and by and large, still

00:08:31.120 --> 00:08:34.210
is true that, if you
order a piece of hardware,

00:08:34.210 --> 00:08:37.460
you get it as the
manufacturer intended it.

00:08:37.460 --> 00:08:40.090
Unfortunately, it's
not always true.

00:08:40.090 --> 00:08:41.799
And there have
been real attacks,

00:08:41.799 --> 00:08:45.920
in the wild, where the supply
chain has been compromised.

00:08:45.920 --> 00:08:48.520
So you're a company.

00:08:48.520 --> 00:08:51.430
You order a piece of hardware,
maybe, a network card

00:08:51.430 --> 00:08:53.600
as shown in this picture.

00:08:53.600 --> 00:08:56.830
And it shows up with malware
already installed on the card.

00:08:56.830 --> 00:08:58.390
Maybe the firmware
has already been

00:08:58.390 --> 00:09:00.490
compromised because
someone inserted themselves

00:09:00.490 --> 00:09:01.695
in the supply chain.

00:09:01.695 --> 00:09:03.300
This is a real
thing that happens.

00:09:03.300 --> 00:09:05.800
And it's something that we,
actually, very actively worry

00:09:05.800 --> 00:09:08.140
about at Google.

00:09:08.140 --> 00:09:10.600
We've taken steps to directly
address this problem.

00:09:10.600 --> 00:09:12.340
We've actually
developed a custom chip,

00:09:12.340 --> 00:09:13.810
which we call Titan--

00:09:13.810 --> 00:09:16.690
which gives us a hardware root
of trust for both machines

00:09:16.690 --> 00:09:18.590
and peripherals.

00:09:18.590 --> 00:09:21.310
So on this network card,
which is the kind of thing

00:09:21.310 --> 00:09:23.020
you might find in
any of our machines

00:09:23.020 --> 00:09:25.810
in our production
network, there's

00:09:25.810 --> 00:09:28.660
actually this little Titan
chip on that card today.

00:09:28.660 --> 00:09:30.880
And what that does is
allows us to verify

00:09:30.880 --> 00:09:33.490
the integrity of all the
firmware and other components

00:09:33.490 --> 00:09:34.600
that are on the card.

00:09:34.600 --> 00:09:36.699
So when it comes
online, we know it

00:09:36.699 --> 00:09:38.990
hasn't been tampered with
relative to where it started.

00:09:43.440 --> 00:09:47.990
So as you might imagine, we have
a lot of machines at Google.

00:09:47.990 --> 00:09:50.929
And you can see them represented
by these little boxes.

00:09:50.929 --> 00:09:52.970
Unfortunately, I'm not
allowed to say exactly how

00:09:52.970 --> 00:09:54.157
many machines we have.

00:09:54.157 --> 00:09:55.490
I'm allowed to give you a range.

00:09:55.490 --> 00:09:58.730
So it's somewhere between
100 and 10 billion.

00:09:58.730 --> 00:10:01.200
That, I can tell you.

00:10:01.200 --> 00:10:04.200
And each one of those machines
can be in a different state,

00:10:04.200 --> 00:10:04.700
right?

00:10:04.700 --> 00:10:07.010
So some might have
just been booted up.

00:10:07.010 --> 00:10:08.960
Some might be running
some software.

00:10:08.960 --> 00:10:10.910
Some might have been
online for a while

00:10:10.910 --> 00:10:13.070
and are at older
version of software.

00:10:13.070 --> 00:10:14.750
How do you kind of
manage these things

00:10:14.750 --> 00:10:19.520
and make sure that things are
in a good state over time?

00:10:19.520 --> 00:10:22.460
Again, here, if we look at what
it takes to start up a machine,

00:10:22.460 --> 00:10:25.730
you turn it on, you load the
BIOS that loads the bootloader

00:10:25.730 --> 00:10:27.210
and so forth.

00:10:27.210 --> 00:10:30.230
We've actually taken steps
at each stage of this process

00:10:30.230 --> 00:10:32.160
to harden each thing.

00:10:32.160 --> 00:10:36.020
So we use Titan at
power-on time to make sure

00:10:36.020 --> 00:10:38.570
that the next thing
is loaded correctly.

00:10:38.570 --> 00:10:41.000
We do our own custom
BIOS where we've actually

00:10:41.000 --> 00:10:43.580
hardened it and made sure
there's no extraneous software.

00:10:43.580 --> 00:10:45.612
We've done the same
thing for the bootloader.

00:10:45.612 --> 00:10:47.570
We've done the same thing
for the Linux kernel.

00:10:47.570 --> 00:10:50.330
So we actually pare
away all the parts

00:10:50.330 --> 00:10:52.100
of the kernel we
don't actually, really

00:10:52.100 --> 00:10:54.380
need to run things
in our data centers.

00:10:54.380 --> 00:10:56.600
And we actively contribute
security improvements

00:10:56.600 --> 00:10:58.596
to the kernel itself.

00:10:58.596 --> 00:11:00.950
And finally, the OS
image, the software

00:11:00.950 --> 00:11:02.790
that runs as a base
image on the platform

00:11:02.790 --> 00:11:05.555
is something that's very
carefully managed and curated.

00:11:08.290 --> 00:11:11.860
At the same time, we keep a
cryptographic, trusted boot

00:11:11.860 --> 00:11:14.420
chain as each stage is loaded.

00:11:14.420 --> 00:11:16.245
So we start from
Titan at the bottom.

00:11:16.245 --> 00:11:17.920
We get the BIOS next.

00:11:17.920 --> 00:11:19.840
And as the BIOS
loads the bootloader,

00:11:19.840 --> 00:11:21.550
and the bootloader
starts the kernel,

00:11:21.550 --> 00:11:23.950
each one signs the next thing.

00:11:23.950 --> 00:11:25.900
And what's so important
about this is,

00:11:25.900 --> 00:11:28.270
when the process is
complete, we know

00:11:28.270 --> 00:11:30.299
that each step was genuine.

00:11:30.299 --> 00:11:31.840
No one could have
changed or inserted

00:11:31.840 --> 00:11:32.840
something along the way.

00:11:36.340 --> 00:11:38.770
A really fundamental
concept in the way

00:11:38.770 --> 00:11:42.940
that we think about security
is that of identity.

00:11:42.940 --> 00:11:46.960
To run a cloud, to run a data
center, you need services,

00:11:46.960 --> 00:11:50.050
you need machines,
and you need people.

00:11:50.050 --> 00:11:51.970
And we assign each
one of these things

00:11:51.970 --> 00:11:53.990
in Identity in our system.

00:11:53.990 --> 00:11:56.740
So we just saw all the
steps that a machine has

00:11:56.740 --> 00:11:59.050
to go through in order to boot.

00:11:59.050 --> 00:12:03.130
Well, what we do is, we only
assign a machine in Identity

00:12:03.130 --> 00:12:05.500
at the end of that process.

00:12:05.500 --> 00:12:07.690
And because virtually
everything works

00:12:07.690 --> 00:12:09.550
by one thing talking
to another thing

00:12:09.550 --> 00:12:12.190
over some
communications channel,

00:12:12.190 --> 00:12:13.900
if you don't have
that identity, you

00:12:13.900 --> 00:12:16.190
don't get to talk
to anything else.

00:12:16.190 --> 00:12:20.110
So only authentic machines
even get to talk to anything.

00:12:20.110 --> 00:12:22.210
Similarly, we apply
the same philosophy

00:12:22.210 --> 00:12:24.340
to services and to people.

00:12:24.340 --> 00:12:26.380
For people to get
an identity, they

00:12:26.380 --> 00:12:29.080
have to pass both a
two-factor authentication

00:12:29.080 --> 00:12:33.370
check, which involves a
secure hardware second factor.

00:12:33.370 --> 00:12:36.660
And they have to be coming
from a trusted managed device.

00:12:36.660 --> 00:12:38.160
And if they don't
pass those things,

00:12:38.160 --> 00:12:39.534
they don't get an
identity, which

00:12:39.534 --> 00:12:41.050
means they can't
talk to machines,

00:12:41.050 --> 00:12:43.510
and they can't talk to services,
which, effectively, means

00:12:43.510 --> 00:12:45.080
they can't do
anything interesting,

00:12:45.080 --> 00:12:47.560
which is as it should be.

00:12:47.560 --> 00:12:50.200
We actually, as it turns out,
do the same thing for services

00:12:50.200 --> 00:12:51.160
as well.

00:12:51.160 --> 00:12:53.535
And we're going to come back
to that in a couple minutes.

00:12:53.535 --> 00:12:55.720
You'll see how we have the
same notion of identity

00:12:55.720 --> 00:13:00.450
for a service and how we
make that notion strong.

00:13:00.450 --> 00:13:02.791
So we talked about how
communication is constant.

00:13:02.791 --> 00:13:05.040
Each of these kinds of things
are talking to the other

00:13:05.040 --> 00:13:08.820
during the normal
operation of a data center.

00:13:08.820 --> 00:13:11.140
And each link is
strongly authenticated.

00:13:11.140 --> 00:13:12.750
So when we say
identity, we actually

00:13:12.750 --> 00:13:15.660
mean giving something a
cryptographic certificate

00:13:15.660 --> 00:13:17.252
that contains its identity.

00:13:17.252 --> 00:13:19.710
And so whenever you have a
link, where one thing is talking

00:13:19.710 --> 00:13:23.460
to another, one party
always has a strong idea

00:13:23.460 --> 00:13:25.009
of who the other party is.

00:13:25.009 --> 00:13:26.550
And it can use that
to make decisions

00:13:26.550 --> 00:13:28.800
about whether it's what
it expected, what it's

00:13:28.800 --> 00:13:30.010
allowed to do, and so forth.

00:13:32.501 --> 00:13:33.000
All right.

00:13:33.000 --> 00:13:35.290
So we've got our data
center up and running.

00:13:35.290 --> 00:13:37.050
We've given
identities to things.

00:13:37.050 --> 00:13:38.130
They're communicating.

00:13:38.130 --> 00:13:39.750
Things are starting to work.

00:13:39.750 --> 00:13:42.116
We're getting some
data in the door.

00:13:42.116 --> 00:13:44.810
How do we manage that data?

00:13:44.810 --> 00:13:47.840
Well, one of the most basic
and important things we can do

00:13:47.840 --> 00:13:50.150
is make sure that
data stays encrypted

00:13:50.150 --> 00:13:53.390
both in transit and at rest.

00:13:53.390 --> 00:13:57.200
All the data coming from
you, as customers, to Google

00:13:57.200 --> 00:13:58.470
is protected with TLS.

00:13:58.470 --> 00:14:00.475
So it's encrypted
in transit there.

00:14:00.475 --> 00:14:03.560
And then, within the data
center and between data centers,

00:14:03.560 --> 00:14:05.840
we actually use a combination
of hardware and software

00:14:05.840 --> 00:14:10.980
encryption to make sure it stays
encrypted across network links.

00:14:10.980 --> 00:14:12.890
When we're about
to store the data--

00:14:12.890 --> 00:14:15.570
in other words, to
repose the data--

00:14:15.570 --> 00:14:18.420
we actually take it, and
we encrypt it in chunks

00:14:18.420 --> 00:14:20.550
with different encryption keys.

00:14:20.550 --> 00:14:24.420
And this kind of ties back to
what we looked at a moment ago.

00:14:24.420 --> 00:14:27.750
Then the question becomes,
who gets to decrypt the data?

00:14:27.750 --> 00:14:30.210
Who gets to have the key?

00:14:30.210 --> 00:14:32.760
And so our highly-secure
Key Management System

00:14:32.760 --> 00:14:36.450
will only hand keys for,
let's say, Cloud Bigtable

00:14:36.450 --> 00:14:40.080
data to the service that
manages Cloud Bigtable.

00:14:40.080 --> 00:14:42.360
And it knows it's that
service because it

00:14:42.360 --> 00:14:44.370
has a very specific identity.

00:14:44.370 --> 00:14:45.990
The Cloud Bigtable
Service's identity

00:14:45.990 --> 00:14:49.050
is distinct from that of
all the other software.

00:14:49.050 --> 00:14:51.480
And that's how we make
sure that data only flows

00:14:51.480 --> 00:14:52.945
in the appropriate direction.

00:14:56.510 --> 00:14:59.720
So I said a minute ago that we
have this notion of identity

00:14:59.720 --> 00:15:02.240
for services as well.

00:15:02.240 --> 00:15:05.210
And this is really critical to
the way that our whole system

00:15:05.210 --> 00:15:08.140
functions.

00:15:08.140 --> 00:15:11.270
So let's come back to our
example of Cloud Bigtable data.

00:15:11.270 --> 00:15:14.120
You create a Cloud
Bigtable incidents.

00:15:14.120 --> 00:15:16.270
We store that somewhere.

00:15:16.270 --> 00:15:19.540
Access to that data in
storage is tightly guarded

00:15:19.540 --> 00:15:21.760
by an Access Control List.

00:15:21.760 --> 00:15:25.660
And we'll list all the machines,
people, services-- anything

00:15:25.660 --> 00:15:27.760
that's allowed to access
it must appear there,

00:15:27.760 --> 00:15:30.300
or the access is not allowed.

00:15:30.300 --> 00:15:31.800
So let's say we
have the system that

00:15:31.800 --> 00:15:35.670
actually serves Cloud Bigtable
data back to you as the user.

00:15:35.670 --> 00:15:37.770
That needs to appear here.

00:15:37.770 --> 00:15:38.950
But what does it mean?

00:15:38.950 --> 00:15:42.390
How do we know what things
ought to be in that list?

00:15:42.390 --> 00:15:45.570
What distinguishes the
Cloud Bigtable serving code

00:15:45.570 --> 00:15:48.540
from any other code?

00:15:48.540 --> 00:15:52.210
The answer is, we have a system
called Binary Authorization

00:15:52.210 --> 00:15:55.350
which is quite novel and
actually, as of today, it's

00:15:55.350 --> 00:15:56.850
something that you
can start to play

00:15:56.850 --> 00:15:59.430
with on Kubernetes on cloud.

00:15:59.430 --> 00:16:02.940
And what it does is it lets us
tie those production service

00:16:02.940 --> 00:16:06.930
identities to specific
pieces of code and processes

00:16:06.930 --> 00:16:09.910
by which that code is
managed and deployed.

00:16:09.910 --> 00:16:12.330
So in our particular
example, we would

00:16:12.330 --> 00:16:15.810
say we only want to put
the Cloud Bigtable service

00:16:15.810 --> 00:16:19.740
identity on the ACL if it
was built from the particular

00:16:19.740 --> 00:16:22.620
build target in our
source repository

00:16:22.620 --> 00:16:26.640
that corresponds to the Cloud
Bigtable serving system.

00:16:26.640 --> 00:16:28.530
And we want to know
that all that code that

00:16:28.530 --> 00:16:33.060
went in there was reviewed by
a second person so one engineer

00:16:33.060 --> 00:16:36.180
couldn't make a mistake and
screw the whole thing up.

00:16:36.180 --> 00:16:38.700
We want to make sure that
code was built by our trusted

00:16:38.700 --> 00:16:39.572
build system.

00:16:39.572 --> 00:16:41.280
We want to make sure
that it was released

00:16:41.280 --> 00:16:45.630
in accordance with our
mandated release process.

00:16:45.630 --> 00:16:48.770
Binary Authorization lets
us do all these things.

00:16:48.770 --> 00:16:50.550
And in fact, we do do them.

00:16:50.550 --> 00:16:54.000
So for somebody to get
access to that user data,

00:16:54.000 --> 00:16:56.040
it has to pass all these steps.

00:16:56.040 --> 00:16:59.010
It has to be code that was
purpose-built to do this one

00:16:59.010 --> 00:16:59.730
thing.

00:16:59.730 --> 00:17:02.070
It has to have been reviewed,
it has to have been built,

00:17:02.070 --> 00:17:04.310
it has to have been deployed
in a proper fashion.

00:17:04.310 --> 00:17:07.200
And finally, it has to present
that strong cryptographic

00:17:07.200 --> 00:17:08.396
identity.

00:17:08.396 --> 00:17:10.020
This is the kind of
thing that gives us

00:17:10.020 --> 00:17:14.975
confidence in the overall
operation of the system.

00:17:14.975 --> 00:17:18.099
So I've given you, actually,
a pretty brief overview

00:17:18.099 --> 00:17:19.369
of our tech stack.

00:17:19.369 --> 00:17:20.935
If you're interested
to learn more,

00:17:20.935 --> 00:17:23.710
and you want to get more
details and information,

00:17:23.710 --> 00:17:26.410
there's actually a fairly
detailed technical white paper

00:17:26.410 --> 00:17:27.460
on our site.

00:17:27.460 --> 00:17:30.520
If you go to
cloud.google.com/security,

00:17:30.520 --> 00:17:32.950
it's called the Security
Infrastructure Whitepaper.

00:17:32.950 --> 00:17:35.560
So I'd encourage anyone who's
interested in learning more

00:17:35.560 --> 00:17:38.250
to take a look.

00:17:38.250 --> 00:17:40.770
All right, so that's
the technology part.

00:17:40.770 --> 00:17:44.240
But as Joe said earlier, you
can't run an entire cloud just

00:17:44.240 --> 00:17:44.920
with technology.

00:17:44.920 --> 00:17:46.760
You need to have operations.

00:17:46.760 --> 00:17:48.920
You need people to
improve the service,

00:17:48.920 --> 00:17:53.230
to maintain the service,
to provide support.

00:17:53.230 --> 00:17:56.230
And our thinking is
that defensive controls

00:17:56.230 --> 00:17:57.940
start in the data center.

00:17:57.940 --> 00:18:03.160
That's literally where the data
is, so you have to protect it.

00:18:03.160 --> 00:18:06.610
And the first line of defense
is physical security, right?

00:18:06.610 --> 00:18:09.250
We actually have multiple
layers of defense.

00:18:09.250 --> 00:18:11.500
We actually have armed guards.

00:18:11.500 --> 00:18:13.900
We have retina scanning.

00:18:13.900 --> 00:18:16.845
We have fingerprint scanning.

00:18:16.845 --> 00:18:18.220
And I know, because
I've actually

00:18:18.220 --> 00:18:19.550
been to a data center floor.

00:18:19.550 --> 00:18:22.400
And let me tell you, it is very
hard to get onto that floor

00:18:22.400 --> 00:18:24.460
if you're not supposed
to be there, especially,

00:18:24.460 --> 00:18:25.640
past those laser beams.

00:18:28.520 --> 00:18:31.770
We have a strict access
management system.

00:18:31.770 --> 00:18:35.180
And what that does is it
says, these specific kinds

00:18:35.180 --> 00:18:37.490
of people, in these
specific job roles

00:18:37.490 --> 00:18:39.500
are allowed onto the
floor at specific times

00:18:39.500 --> 00:18:41.600
for a specific reason, right?

00:18:41.600 --> 00:18:45.230
So again, it's difficult
to get onto the floor

00:18:45.230 --> 00:18:47.600
to even be near the machines
where the data lives,

00:18:47.600 --> 00:18:49.591
unless you have a very
good reason to be there,

00:18:49.591 --> 00:18:50.840
and we know what you're doing.

00:18:50.840 --> 00:18:53.270
And finally, we
retrospectively look

00:18:53.270 --> 00:18:56.244
at who, in fact, was out
there, why they were there.

00:18:56.244 --> 00:18:58.160
And we audit that to
make sure that everything

00:18:58.160 --> 00:18:59.632
was appropriate.

00:18:59.632 --> 00:19:01.090
JOSEPH VALENTE:
Did you have to get

00:19:01.090 --> 00:19:03.155
past the laser beams to
get on the floor as well?

00:19:03.155 --> 00:19:04.030
UMESH SHANKAR: I did.

00:19:04.030 --> 00:19:04.090
I did.

00:19:04.090 --> 00:19:06.220
I studied contortionism
for about six months.

00:19:06.220 --> 00:19:09.342
But if you've seen the
movie "Mr. &amp; Mrs. Smith,"

00:19:09.342 --> 00:19:10.300
it was a lot like that.

00:19:10.300 --> 00:19:11.200
JOSEPH VALENTE: OK, cool.

00:19:11.200 --> 00:19:12.033
UMESH SHANKAR: Yeah.

00:19:12.033 --> 00:19:12.695
[LAUGHTER]

00:19:15.900 --> 00:19:20.230
So we said that we need people
to administer our cloud, right?

00:19:20.230 --> 00:19:22.180
And that means that
those people themselves

00:19:22.180 --> 00:19:24.730
can become targets, right?

00:19:24.730 --> 00:19:29.161
If I'm an attacker, if I want
to gain access to Google's data,

00:19:29.161 --> 00:19:30.160
how am I going to do it?

00:19:30.160 --> 00:19:34.360
Well, I might go after someone
who works at Google, right?

00:19:34.360 --> 00:19:37.480
So to protect our employees,
we use those two techniques

00:19:37.480 --> 00:19:38.690
I mentioned before.

00:19:38.690 --> 00:19:41.110
We have phishing-resistant
hardware authentication

00:19:41.110 --> 00:19:44.530
in the form of security keys,
which is something we recommend

00:19:44.530 --> 00:19:46.500
that everybody use.

00:19:46.500 --> 00:19:48.250
And it's something
which, on cloud,

00:19:48.250 --> 00:19:52.390
you can actually mandate that
all your employees use as well.

00:19:52.390 --> 00:19:54.700
And we mandate the use of
managed access devices.

00:19:54.700 --> 00:19:58.060
So you can't just pick
up a random laptop

00:19:58.060 --> 00:20:00.580
and start debugging
Google Cloud software.

00:20:00.580 --> 00:20:02.710
You have to come from a
specific device that's

00:20:02.710 --> 00:20:06.140
been provisioned by Google and
managed to be in a good state.

00:20:09.400 --> 00:20:09.900
OK.

00:20:09.900 --> 00:20:12.540
So you're a person at Google.

00:20:12.540 --> 00:20:16.920
You need to do some support
work or administrative work.

00:20:16.920 --> 00:20:20.124
We still want to make sure
that only the right people have

00:20:20.124 --> 00:20:21.540
access to things,
and they're only

00:20:21.540 --> 00:20:23.220
doing things for
the right reason.

00:20:23.220 --> 00:20:26.310
So here, too, we have
role-based access controls.

00:20:26.310 --> 00:20:29.940
If you don't work on Cloud
Bigtable in a support role,

00:20:29.940 --> 00:20:31.440
you're not going
to ever have access

00:20:31.440 --> 00:20:34.930
to Cloud Bigtable resources.

00:20:34.930 --> 00:20:37.320
Our support tools
are purpose-built.

00:20:37.320 --> 00:20:40.830
So if you work in support,
the only way for you

00:20:40.830 --> 00:20:44.910
to gain access to, say,
metadata about a Bigtable

00:20:44.910 --> 00:20:46.800
is to use this support tool.

00:20:46.800 --> 00:20:48.540
And the support
tool will require

00:20:48.540 --> 00:20:51.840
you to say why you're
doing something.

00:20:51.840 --> 00:20:54.930
So if you can't produce the
specific customer support

00:20:54.930 --> 00:20:57.109
ticket, as an example--

00:20:57.109 --> 00:20:59.400
that is the thing that you're
following up on to access

00:20:59.400 --> 00:21:02.730
that specific resource--
you're not going to get access.

00:21:02.730 --> 00:21:06.490
And finally, again, we employ
auditing very extensively.

00:21:06.490 --> 00:21:08.250
It's a good way
for us to make sure

00:21:08.250 --> 00:21:10.650
that people are using that
access appropriately all

00:21:10.650 --> 00:21:13.212
the time.

00:21:13.212 --> 00:21:14.670
So let's come back
to that question

00:21:14.670 --> 00:21:16.590
that Joe raised early on.

00:21:16.590 --> 00:21:18.990
"Am I secure in the cloud?"

00:21:18.990 --> 00:21:21.310
I mean, I can't answer
that question for you.

00:21:21.310 --> 00:21:22.410
But I can say--

00:21:22.410 --> 00:21:24.450
and hopefully, you
have a sense now--

00:21:24.450 --> 00:21:28.410
that we have made an enormous
investment in security.

00:21:28.410 --> 00:21:30.690
We have, I think,
over 900 people

00:21:30.690 --> 00:21:32.880
working on security
and privacy alone.

00:21:32.880 --> 00:21:34.710
And the only way that
we're able to make

00:21:34.710 --> 00:21:36.810
this kind of an
investment is because we

00:21:36.810 --> 00:21:38.700
operate at such a large scale.

00:21:38.700 --> 00:21:40.980
It actually makes financial
sense for us to do this.

00:21:40.980 --> 00:21:42.390
We have so much at stake.

00:21:42.390 --> 00:21:45.195
And we can amortize that
cost over a very large number

00:21:45.195 --> 00:21:47.490
of machines.

00:21:47.490 --> 00:21:51.400
Now, this is something that
customers can do on their own

00:21:51.400 --> 00:21:52.240
in part.

00:21:52.240 --> 00:21:53.880
But it's very
expensive to do that.

00:21:53.880 --> 00:21:57.050
So we actually think
that, for most customers,

00:21:57.050 --> 00:22:00.030
they're going to end up having
the same or better security

00:22:00.030 --> 00:22:02.340
as they move to a public
cloud by taking advantage

00:22:02.340 --> 00:22:08.840
of these kind of sophisticated
features that we've built.

00:22:08.840 --> 00:22:12.190
So that actually brings us
to pillar number three--

00:22:12.190 --> 00:22:14.480
accountability.

00:22:14.480 --> 00:22:16.520
It's well and good
for me to talk to you

00:22:16.520 --> 00:22:18.620
about our technology,
for me to talk

00:22:18.620 --> 00:22:21.530
to you about our operations,
for us to publish whitepapers.

00:22:21.530 --> 00:22:23.960
I think these are actually
all very important things.

00:22:23.960 --> 00:22:26.810
We're trying to be as open and
transparent as we can about

00:22:26.810 --> 00:22:31.910
how we run our cloud so you can
have that as a basis for trust.

00:22:31.910 --> 00:22:33.590
But we think it's
really important, too,

00:22:33.590 --> 00:22:35.720
to give you direct
accountability.

00:22:35.720 --> 00:22:37.970
What is Google actually doing?

00:22:37.970 --> 00:22:40.019
Why are they doing it?

00:22:40.019 --> 00:22:41.810
And the reason we think
that's so important

00:22:41.810 --> 00:22:43.915
is we have to hold
ourselves accountable.

00:22:43.915 --> 00:22:47.420
We understand why
this is so important.

00:22:47.420 --> 00:22:50.360
When you run a
system like Gmail,

00:22:50.360 --> 00:22:53.210
you worry about accidental
exposure of data.

00:22:53.210 --> 00:22:56.810
You worry about unmonitored
access by admins.

00:22:56.810 --> 00:22:59.490
This is something that
we do all the time.

00:22:59.490 --> 00:23:02.330
And the way we hold ourselves
accountable beyond all

00:23:02.330 --> 00:23:05.630
those access controls,
which I mentioned, is we

00:23:05.630 --> 00:23:08.390
have these robust internal logs.

00:23:08.390 --> 00:23:11.390
We collect detailed logs of
whenever people at Google

00:23:11.390 --> 00:23:14.460
do things, we funnel them
into a central location,

00:23:14.460 --> 00:23:16.490
and we apply very
sophisticated techniques

00:23:16.490 --> 00:23:20.720
to try to detect if anything
was being done inappropriately.

00:23:20.720 --> 00:23:22.790
If we didn't have
those kind of controls,

00:23:22.790 --> 00:23:25.730
we couldn't feel comfortable
running those kinds of services

00:23:25.730 --> 00:23:27.575
and feeling like they
were actually secure.

00:23:30.150 --> 00:23:31.830
So it's a fair
question, as a customer

00:23:31.830 --> 00:23:33.870
to say, all right,
well, you want

00:23:33.870 --> 00:23:37.320
me to trust you to run my
business on Google Cloud.

00:23:37.320 --> 00:23:39.617
But I don't have access to
those same internal logs

00:23:39.617 --> 00:23:40.200
that you have.

00:23:40.200 --> 00:23:43.680
How am I supposed
to feel comfortable?

00:23:43.680 --> 00:23:45.810
Well, it's exactly to
answer that question

00:23:45.810 --> 00:23:48.990
that I'm really excited to
announce Access Transparency.

00:23:48.990 --> 00:23:51.060
It's a new product
offering on GCP.

00:23:51.060 --> 00:23:52.560
It's something that
you're not going

00:23:52.560 --> 00:23:54.630
to find on any other cloud.

00:23:54.630 --> 00:23:57.550
And the point of it is to
give you meaningful oversight

00:23:57.550 --> 00:23:58.860
of your cloud provider.

00:23:58.860 --> 00:24:01.530
We're going to give
you near real-time logs

00:24:01.530 --> 00:24:04.415
when a person at Google
accesses your content.

00:24:04.415 --> 00:24:06.780
We think this is a
really important step

00:24:06.780 --> 00:24:09.090
in establishing trust
with you, the customer,

00:24:09.090 --> 00:24:11.350
and giving you a basis
on which you can build

00:24:11.350 --> 00:24:14.690
a secure business in the cloud.

00:24:14.690 --> 00:24:17.540
And I think the best
way to understand

00:24:17.540 --> 00:24:19.970
the concept behind
Access Transparency

00:24:19.970 --> 00:24:22.910
is to look at an example.

00:24:22.910 --> 00:24:26.420
By far, the most common
reason anyone at Google

00:24:26.420 --> 00:24:30.170
would ever have to access
customer content is

00:24:30.170 --> 00:24:31.150
for support.

00:24:31.150 --> 00:24:34.680
It's when we're asked to do so.

00:24:34.680 --> 00:24:36.310
So let's look at an example.

00:24:36.310 --> 00:24:38.690
This is the kind of thing
that certainly happens.

00:24:38.690 --> 00:24:42.980
A customer is a user of our
Google Cloud Storage API.

00:24:42.980 --> 00:24:45.740
They try to read one of
the objects in a bucket,

00:24:45.740 --> 00:24:47.960
and the data looks
corrupted, and they're not

00:24:47.960 --> 00:24:53.300
able to figure out what was
going on, so they call support.

00:24:53.300 --> 00:24:57.260
Now, that action by
the customer admin

00:24:57.260 --> 00:24:59.900
will generate a Cloud Audit Log.

00:24:59.900 --> 00:25:01.550
So when we talk
about accountability,

00:25:01.550 --> 00:25:03.680
there's layers of
accountability.

00:25:03.680 --> 00:25:05.930
So as a customer,
you want to keep tabs

00:25:05.930 --> 00:25:07.672
on what your own
admins are doing.

00:25:07.672 --> 00:25:09.380
And Cloud Audit Logging
lets you do that.

00:25:09.380 --> 00:25:10.921
You could see that
someone was trying

00:25:10.921 --> 00:25:14.475
to read this particular object.

00:25:14.475 --> 00:25:16.290
So now let's look
at the next level.

00:25:16.290 --> 00:25:18.425
They contacted Google Support.

00:25:18.425 --> 00:25:21.540
And so the support
person goes in.

00:25:21.540 --> 00:25:24.480
They're not able to access
the content of the object,

00:25:24.480 --> 00:25:27.930
because that's just not allowed
by our policy in general.

00:25:27.930 --> 00:25:30.671
But they are able to access
things like the Access Control

00:25:30.671 --> 00:25:31.170
List.

00:25:31.170 --> 00:25:33.744
Because sometimes maybe
the problem is there,

00:25:33.744 --> 00:25:35.160
or maybe it's with
other metadata,

00:25:35.160 --> 00:25:38.350
and they can figure it out
without having to go deeper.

00:25:38.350 --> 00:25:41.190
But in this case,
everything looks fine.

00:25:41.190 --> 00:25:42.570
They had to say why they did it.

00:25:42.570 --> 00:25:45.010
It was to handle this particular
customer support ticket--

00:25:45.010 --> 00:25:46.470
12345.

00:25:46.470 --> 00:25:47.910
And it all looks fine.

00:25:47.910 --> 00:25:51.690
And they're going to have
to escalate to engineering.

00:25:51.690 --> 00:25:55.290
Now, before Access
Transparency, this

00:25:55.290 --> 00:25:57.300
is what you would get
to see as a customer

00:25:57.300 --> 00:25:59.850
when that access occurred--

00:25:59.850 --> 00:26:01.800
pretty much a gray box, right?

00:26:01.800 --> 00:26:04.290
That's not very
satisfying, right?

00:26:04.290 --> 00:26:07.540
With Access Transparency,
it looks very different.

00:26:07.540 --> 00:26:09.515
You're going to get a
log in near real time--

00:26:09.515 --> 00:26:12.680
so we're talking
minutes, not days--

00:26:12.680 --> 00:26:14.760
of what specific
thing was accessed.

00:26:14.760 --> 00:26:15.900
And you can see here.

00:26:15.900 --> 00:26:17.820
It's the particular
object that was accessed.

00:26:17.820 --> 00:26:19.444
And at the end, you
can see it was just

00:26:19.444 --> 00:26:23.370
the ACL, not the content
of the object itself.

00:26:23.370 --> 00:26:25.950
You get to see what type
of action was performed,

00:26:25.950 --> 00:26:29.670
and you get to see the reason
for that access, right?

00:26:29.670 --> 00:26:32.100
Because the point
of this is to say,

00:26:32.100 --> 00:26:35.160
yes, I can account for
each thing that occurred.

00:26:35.160 --> 00:26:36.120
This makes sense to me.

00:26:36.120 --> 00:26:37.328
I understand why it happened.

00:26:40.200 --> 00:26:41.880
So let's go to the
next level, right?

00:26:41.880 --> 00:26:43.990
We couldn't resolve the
problem with support.

00:26:43.990 --> 00:26:45.650
We had to get
engineering involved.

00:26:45.650 --> 00:26:49.200
And engineering has access to
lower-level debugging tools

00:26:49.200 --> 00:26:51.600
than support
typically does, right?

00:26:51.600 --> 00:26:53.790
So they can actually go
into the underlying systems

00:26:53.790 --> 00:26:55.050
where the things live.

00:26:55.050 --> 00:26:58.650
They can use the internal
APIs to try to pull more data,

00:26:58.650 --> 00:27:00.761
to figure out what's going on.

00:27:00.761 --> 00:27:01.260
All right.

00:27:01.260 --> 00:27:03.210
So in this case,
again, the engineer

00:27:03.210 --> 00:27:04.630
has to say a justification.

00:27:04.630 --> 00:27:06.630
So they're doing it
for this reason--

00:27:06.630 --> 00:27:09.000
this particular ticket number.

00:27:09.000 --> 00:27:11.130
And they're able to get
the data and say, OK, I

00:27:11.130 --> 00:27:12.750
see what happened.

00:27:12.750 --> 00:27:14.310
The data had a
different encoding

00:27:14.310 --> 00:27:16.018
than the one that you
thought, and that's

00:27:16.018 --> 00:27:18.000
why the data appeared
to be corrupted.

00:27:18.000 --> 00:27:19.320
We're able to recover.

00:27:19.320 --> 00:27:24.980
And so everything is good,
notify the customer, great.

00:27:24.980 --> 00:27:28.850
Importantly, you're going to get
another log when this happens.

00:27:28.850 --> 00:27:30.530
And this time, you
can actually see

00:27:30.530 --> 00:27:35.870
that the content of the
object was accessed, right?

00:27:35.870 --> 00:27:38.450
That's the level of
granularity and transparency we

00:27:38.450 --> 00:27:40.600
think is really important
for us to offer.

00:27:44.810 --> 00:27:47.780
I'd like to spend just
one more minute talking

00:27:47.780 --> 00:27:51.290
about what I like to
call deep transparency

00:27:51.290 --> 00:27:55.008
versus shallow transparency.

00:27:55.008 --> 00:27:58.570
So I mentioned that
support accounts

00:27:58.570 --> 00:28:04.540
for the vast majority of
accesses, that support

00:28:04.540 --> 00:28:06.289
requested by the customer is--

00:28:06.289 --> 00:28:07.580
I don't know the exact figures.

00:28:07.580 --> 00:28:11.420
But it's like 80%, 90%,
something like that.

00:28:11.420 --> 00:28:14.120
And so that seems like,
oh, it's not that hard.

00:28:14.120 --> 00:28:15.890
We have a purpose-built
support tool.

00:28:15.890 --> 00:28:19.270
Let's go instrument the tool,
we'll get some logs, and we'll?

00:28:19.270 --> 00:28:23.230
Send them over to the
customer, no big deal, right?

00:28:23.230 --> 00:28:26.260
And that is definitely way
better than nothing, right?

00:28:26.260 --> 00:28:28.720
It turns out, actually, not
to be that easy, in the end,

00:28:28.720 --> 00:28:31.720
but you could do it.

00:28:31.720 --> 00:28:34.020
We decided to go beyond that.

00:28:34.020 --> 00:28:37.470
We wanted to say, OK, even
though we could give you

00:28:37.470 --> 00:28:41.880
80% of the logs for 10% of the
effort, that doesn't really

00:28:41.880 --> 00:28:43.680
fulfill the point
of this feature.

00:28:43.680 --> 00:28:46.050
The point of this
feature is to tell you

00:28:46.050 --> 00:28:49.440
as much as possible about every
time that someone at Google

00:28:49.440 --> 00:28:51.116
accessed your content.

00:28:51.116 --> 00:28:54.900
And so we wanted to get
those deeper access points,

00:28:54.900 --> 00:28:58.350
those internal APIs, those
low-level general purpose

00:28:58.350 --> 00:29:01.590
storage systems to be
part of the solution too.

00:29:01.590 --> 00:29:04.680
Now, it turns out there
is way more code--

00:29:04.680 --> 00:29:07.680
there are a lot more systems
there than in our support

00:29:07.680 --> 00:29:09.150
system, right?

00:29:09.150 --> 00:29:13.900
It's an order of magnitude
more stuff, maybe even more.

00:29:13.900 --> 00:29:20.170
And it only gives us 10%, 5% of
the logs, something like that.

00:29:20.170 --> 00:29:21.835
But it felt like the
right thing to do.

00:29:21.835 --> 00:29:23.460
And that's the kind
of thing that we're

00:29:23.460 --> 00:29:26.850
hoping to do going forward and
bring it to all of GCP for you.

00:29:30.070 --> 00:29:30.570
OK.

00:29:30.570 --> 00:29:32.570
With that, I'm going to
hand it back over to Joe

00:29:32.570 --> 00:29:34.600
to show you a demo of
the system in action.

00:29:34.600 --> 00:29:35.850
JOSEPH VALENTE: Thanks, Umesh.

00:29:38.790 --> 00:29:39.370
OK.

00:29:39.370 --> 00:29:41.920
So let's take a look at how
Access Transparency would

00:29:41.920 --> 00:29:44.510
actually work in real life.

00:29:44.510 --> 00:29:48.790
So let's slow this down a little
bit so it's easier for everyone

00:29:48.790 --> 00:29:49.650
here to see.

00:29:49.650 --> 00:29:52.032
I'm going to slow it
down to half speed.

00:29:52.032 --> 00:29:53.240
Ooh, I don't want to do that.

00:29:56.390 --> 00:29:57.980
And so I'm going to go ahead.

00:29:57.980 --> 00:29:59.452
And I'm going to
open up a bucket.

00:29:59.452 --> 00:30:01.160
And I'm going to try
and access the file.

00:30:01.160 --> 00:30:02.535
And I get this
error, "Permission

00:30:02.535 --> 00:30:03.889
denied on Cloud KMS key."

00:30:03.889 --> 00:30:04.680
So that's not good.

00:30:04.680 --> 00:30:06.471
I'm going to go and
contact support and see

00:30:06.471 --> 00:30:08.750
if they can help me out.

00:30:08.750 --> 00:30:11.330
So this is not exactly what
the support person sees,

00:30:11.330 --> 00:30:14.090
but it's, roundabout, exactly
how the experience works.

00:30:14.090 --> 00:30:17.240
Support person inserts
this justification.

00:30:17.240 --> 00:30:19.496
It says case number 202123.

00:30:19.496 --> 00:30:21.620
Now try and remember that
case number, because it's

00:30:21.620 --> 00:30:22.720
going to come back later.

00:30:22.720 --> 00:30:25.220
I'm not testing you.

00:30:25.220 --> 00:30:26.870
So they insert
that justification,

00:30:26.870 --> 00:30:27.810
and it's checked.

00:30:27.810 --> 00:30:30.720
It says, OK, this person
has that ticket open.

00:30:30.720 --> 00:30:32.290
I'm going to give them access.

00:30:32.290 --> 00:30:34.790
And they actually end up saying
to you there was this issue,

00:30:34.790 --> 00:30:39.830
and you fix it, and the
data is showing up fine,

00:30:39.830 --> 00:30:40.700
so that's great.

00:30:40.700 --> 00:30:43.970
So you're going to say,
OK, I fixed my issue.

00:30:43.970 --> 00:30:49.230
Let me go to the Logging panel
and see what logs showed up.

00:30:49.230 --> 00:30:51.980
And now, you can see that,
in the Logging panel,

00:30:51.980 --> 00:30:54.050
there's Access
Transparency logs available

00:30:54.050 --> 00:30:56.210
because you contacted support.

00:30:56.210 --> 00:30:58.940
So we can go ahead and
bring those logs up.

00:30:58.940 --> 00:31:01.192
And I'm going to
expand this panel

00:31:01.192 --> 00:31:03.650
because I want to see a little
bit more detail about what's

00:31:03.650 --> 00:31:04.985
inside these logs.

00:31:04.985 --> 00:31:06.470
I'm going to pick this log here.

00:31:06.470 --> 00:31:09.790
I go and take a closer
look, and there it is.

00:31:09.790 --> 00:31:12.620
I've got information about
exactly what happened.

00:31:12.620 --> 00:31:14.820
And most importantly,
here, I've got the reason.

00:31:14.820 --> 00:31:17.630
So there is that case
number again, 202123--

00:31:17.630 --> 00:31:22.510
so the full, sort of audit
pathway showing me everything.

00:31:22.510 --> 00:31:24.590
Best of all, it works
with all my features

00:31:24.590 --> 00:31:25.580
in Stackdriver Logging.

00:31:25.580 --> 00:31:28.220
So I can set up metrics,
I can set up alerting,

00:31:28.220 --> 00:31:30.022
I can get it to
send me an email.

00:31:30.022 --> 00:31:32.230
And we have another session
on this tomorrow morning,

00:31:32.230 --> 00:31:35.420
Sect 211 at 9:00 AM if you want
to learn more about how exactly

00:31:35.420 --> 00:31:37.115
this can be accomplished.

00:31:37.115 --> 00:31:38.990
But for now, the only
important thing to know

00:31:38.990 --> 00:31:43.820
is it works just like any
other log on Stackdriver.

00:31:43.820 --> 00:31:46.220
So that was a little
bit hard to see.

00:31:46.220 --> 00:31:50.840
So maybe let's jump out of this.

00:31:50.840 --> 00:31:55.240
And let's take a look at--

00:31:55.240 --> 00:31:59.100
let's maybe maximize that.

00:31:59.100 --> 00:32:01.570
Let's take a look at this log
in a little bit more detail.

00:32:01.570 --> 00:32:05.070
So I want to zoom in to the top
part of our Access Transparency

00:32:05.070 --> 00:32:07.560
log and just talk through
the information that's

00:32:07.560 --> 00:32:10.350
contained inside it.

00:32:10.350 --> 00:32:12.900
So the first line there is Type.

00:32:12.900 --> 00:32:16.710
And you can clearly see that
it says Transparency Log.

00:32:16.710 --> 00:32:18.510
Why is that useful?

00:32:18.510 --> 00:32:21.630
If we want to go ahead and
set up alerts or monitoring

00:32:21.630 --> 00:32:23.610
on these particular
types of logs,

00:32:23.610 --> 00:32:26.895
if we want to go and fire off an
email to an auditor that says,

00:32:26.895 --> 00:32:28.770
please check exactly
what happened every time

00:32:28.770 --> 00:32:31.650
we see one of these things, we
can use this field to match,

00:32:31.650 --> 00:32:34.940
so we can see that it's
a Transparency log.

00:32:34.940 --> 00:32:37.010
The second section gives
a bit more information

00:32:37.010 --> 00:32:40.800
about the Google employee
who made the access.

00:32:40.800 --> 00:32:43.220
So you can clearly
see here that we've

00:32:43.220 --> 00:32:47.660
got the principalOfficeCountry
that the person is typically

00:32:47.660 --> 00:32:49.350
located in.

00:32:49.350 --> 00:32:50.972
We also have the
principalPhysica

00:32:50.972 --> 00:32:52.155
lLocationCountry.

00:32:52.155 --> 00:32:54.950
So that is a special
field that is

00:32:54.950 --> 00:32:58.010
going to tell you exactly where
the person making the access

00:32:58.010 --> 00:33:00.955
was at the time that
the access was made.

00:33:00.955 --> 00:33:02.330
We've got some
special technology

00:33:02.330 --> 00:33:05.640
that allows us to be able
to make that determination.

00:33:05.640 --> 00:33:08.570
And then it also, tells us the
employing entity of the Google.

00:33:08.570 --> 00:33:11.030
So it tells us here that
that Google employee

00:33:11.030 --> 00:33:12.650
works for the Google LLC.

00:33:12.650 --> 00:33:14.480
That's our American entity.

00:33:14.480 --> 00:33:18.086
And this might be useful for you
if you have specific, sort of,

00:33:18.086 --> 00:33:19.850
governance risk and
compliance needs

00:33:19.850 --> 00:33:23.580
for this particular information.

00:33:23.580 --> 00:33:25.550
So the second part of the log.

00:33:25.550 --> 00:33:26.990
This gives a bit
more information

00:33:26.990 --> 00:33:29.245
about exactly what was accessed.

00:33:29.245 --> 00:33:32.435
So here you can
see Cloud Storage.

00:33:32.435 --> 00:33:35.140
There was a read executed on it.

00:33:35.140 --> 00:33:39.210
And the resource name was
this particular bucket, /acl,

00:33:39.210 --> 00:33:41.420
which means Access Control List.

00:33:41.420 --> 00:33:43.910
So I can identify that
the person in question

00:33:43.910 --> 00:33:45.310
didn't look at the content.

00:33:45.310 --> 00:33:49.181
They were looking at the Access
Control List of this object.

00:33:49.181 --> 00:33:52.970
And finally, reason--
so I can see here

00:33:52.970 --> 00:33:55.816
that the reason is given.

00:33:55.816 --> 00:33:58.460
It's clearly specified
what the case number is.

00:33:58.460 --> 00:34:01.190
And I can go ahead and
pull that case number up

00:34:01.190 --> 00:34:04.310
and perform an audit against it
to see if it's what I expected.

00:34:04.310 --> 00:34:06.380
If I opened up a
ticket that said,

00:34:06.380 --> 00:34:10.130
please investigate why I
can't access my Cloud Storage,

00:34:10.130 --> 00:34:12.552
and then I had a
bunch of all these

00:34:12.552 --> 00:34:14.510
logs going on, and on,
and on, and on for days,

00:34:14.510 --> 00:34:16.783
I might be like,
why did that happen?

00:34:16.783 --> 00:34:18.199
So that gives you
that audit trail

00:34:18.199 --> 00:34:21.440
that is able to connect
everything together.

00:34:21.440 --> 00:34:24.440
You'll notice that there's
also another field called Type.

00:34:24.440 --> 00:34:28.460
So the Type specifies that it
is a customer-initiated support

00:34:28.460 --> 00:34:29.750
access.

00:34:29.750 --> 00:34:32.300
And as Umesh mentioned,
the absolute vast majority

00:34:32.300 --> 00:34:35.179
of all the accesses
are customer-initiated.

00:34:35.179 --> 00:34:39.270
But there are a few cases
in which they may not be.

00:34:39.270 --> 00:34:41.730
In our documentation,
which you can find online,

00:34:41.730 --> 00:34:43.440
we talk a little
bit more in detail

00:34:43.440 --> 00:34:47.269
about exactly why we might
otherwise access your data.

00:34:47.269 --> 00:34:50.610
So I spoke about
customer-initiated support.

00:34:50.610 --> 00:34:51.303
You contact us.

00:34:51.303 --> 00:34:53.219
You say, hey, I need
some help with something.

00:34:53.219 --> 00:34:55.270
That's the most common one.

00:34:55.270 --> 00:34:57.330
There's also
Google-initiated service.

00:34:57.330 --> 00:34:59.550
Now, this is, by far, the
second-most common one.

00:34:59.550 --> 00:35:01.830
And it's where there's
a bug, or an outage,

00:35:01.830 --> 00:35:04.970
or some issue where we detect
that there's a problem,

00:35:04.970 --> 00:35:07.600
and we need to go and fix it.

00:35:07.600 --> 00:35:10.060
Then there's third-party
data request.

00:35:10.060 --> 00:35:12.690
And that's to do with things
like court-ordered accesses

00:35:12.690 --> 00:35:15.040
or legal accesses.

00:35:15.040 --> 00:35:17.590
And finally, there's
Google-initiated review.

00:35:17.590 --> 00:35:21.010
And that's to do with things
like abuse or security reviews.

00:35:21.010 --> 00:35:23.140
And I note that you may
have custom terms that

00:35:23.140 --> 00:35:27.400
allow you to opt out of some of
those particular review cases.

00:35:27.400 --> 00:35:29.650
I'm not going to go into
detail and read all of these.

00:35:29.650 --> 00:35:32.191
I'd encourage you to head to
our documentation to learn more.

00:35:32.191 --> 00:35:34.680
But I just wanted to highlight
that if one of these four

00:35:34.680 --> 00:35:37.515
reasons isn't present, then the
data will never be accessed.

00:35:40.540 --> 00:35:44.790
So Access Transparency--
what are the key features?

00:35:44.790 --> 00:35:46.880
What does it cover?

00:35:46.880 --> 00:35:50.190
Think about it as covering
all the data you give us.

00:35:50.190 --> 00:35:53.080
So unless it's a public
resource identifier,

00:35:53.080 --> 00:35:57.080
like a GCS bucket name, we will
give you an Access Transparency

00:35:57.080 --> 00:35:59.060
log when it gets accessed.

00:35:59.060 --> 00:36:00.560
And that's important because

00:36:00.560 --> 00:36:03.650
Even something as small
as the name of a VM,

00:36:03.650 --> 00:36:06.289
or a label that you've
attached to a VM,

00:36:06.289 --> 00:36:08.580
we're going to treat that
like it's your customer data.

00:36:08.580 --> 00:36:10.850
And we're going to give
you a log of it whenever

00:36:10.850 --> 00:36:12.170
it's accessed.

00:36:12.170 --> 00:36:13.610
And we did that
because we didn't

00:36:13.610 --> 00:36:15.560
want to make value
judgments about what

00:36:15.560 --> 00:36:18.486
it is that you find to be
important or not important.

00:36:18.486 --> 00:36:20.360
We just want to give
you as much transparency

00:36:20.360 --> 00:36:22.610
as we can over your
content and then what's

00:36:22.610 --> 00:36:25.430
happening to it on the cloud.

00:36:25.430 --> 00:36:28.730
Timeliness-- you're going to get
these logs in near real time.

00:36:28.730 --> 00:36:33.014
As Umesh said, it's
minutes, not days.

00:36:33.014 --> 00:36:35.430
Availability-- you can get it
through Stackdriver Logging,

00:36:35.430 --> 00:36:35.975
as we saw.

00:36:35.975 --> 00:36:38.730
And you can also get it
through our Cloud Logging APIs

00:36:38.730 --> 00:36:43.510
and through a new offering
from SAP called Data Custodian.

00:36:43.510 --> 00:36:45.520
These logs have
audit-grade protections.

00:36:45.520 --> 00:36:46.937
So similar to your
audit logs, you

00:36:46.937 --> 00:36:48.894
can't delete them unless
you delete the project

00:36:48.894 --> 00:36:49.750
or the organization.

00:36:49.750 --> 00:36:51.040
That gives you that
extra protection

00:36:51.040 --> 00:36:52.831
that a bad actor hasn't
gone in and removed

00:36:52.831 --> 00:36:55.050
records of what's happened.

00:36:55.050 --> 00:36:59.780
And retention-- we hold
them for free for 13 months.

00:36:59.780 --> 00:37:01.400
Now, some additional
important details

00:37:01.400 --> 00:37:03.910
are that Access
Transparency logs

00:37:03.910 --> 00:37:08.550
cover human accesses through
support and debugging pathways.

00:37:08.550 --> 00:37:12.920
So in these cases, you'll
get your real-time logs.

00:37:12.920 --> 00:37:16.060
Accesses that are made pursuant
to legal investigations

00:37:16.060 --> 00:37:18.176
will typically surface
in near real time.

00:37:18.176 --> 00:37:19.550
However, there
may be cases where

00:37:19.550 --> 00:37:21.890
we're prohibited, by law,
from servicing those,

00:37:21.890 --> 00:37:24.940
and they won't show up.

00:37:24.940 --> 00:37:26.530
Then there's system access.

00:37:26.530 --> 00:37:28.790
And these are things
like indexing jobs.

00:37:28.790 --> 00:37:31.570
But they could be other
jobs that run in our system.

00:37:31.570 --> 00:37:35.320
As Umesh explained before, we
have this comprehensive system

00:37:35.320 --> 00:37:38.320
of binary authorization
that verifies

00:37:38.320 --> 00:37:41.590
that the code that's touching
this data in Bigtable

00:37:41.590 --> 00:37:43.960
comes from the Bigtable service.

00:37:43.960 --> 00:37:45.850
And because of
those controls, we

00:37:45.850 --> 00:37:47.680
don't generate Access
Transparency logs

00:37:47.680 --> 00:37:52.170
in those cases because we have
Binary Authorization in place.

00:37:52.170 --> 00:37:55.500
And then, finally, accesses
to very low-level storage

00:37:55.500 --> 00:37:56.580
systems--

00:37:56.580 --> 00:37:58.120
these are extremely rare--

00:37:58.120 --> 00:38:01.010
haven't seen one yet, but these
are not real time at present.

00:38:01.010 --> 00:38:02.760
There's a human in the
loop at the moment.

00:38:02.760 --> 00:38:04.885
We will have real-time
availability, though, in Q4.

00:38:07.700 --> 00:38:11.780
So you may be asking, at
this point, what's new?

00:38:11.780 --> 00:38:15.102
We've already detailed the fact
that we don't access your data

00:38:15.102 --> 00:38:17.060
for reasons other than
necessary to provide you

00:38:17.060 --> 00:38:18.920
a service in our
terms of service.

00:38:18.920 --> 00:38:20.750
And what Access
Transparency just does

00:38:20.750 --> 00:38:22.760
is give you another
layer of visibility

00:38:22.760 --> 00:38:24.470
over that commitment.

00:38:24.470 --> 00:38:27.170
And it's part of a long list
of transparency commitments

00:38:27.170 --> 00:38:30.600
we've been making at
Google over many years.

00:38:30.600 --> 00:38:32.240
These include our
tech whitepapers

00:38:32.240 --> 00:38:33.864
that you can go ahead
and read to learn

00:38:33.864 --> 00:38:37.594
about how our platform works,
our compliance certifications.

00:38:37.594 --> 00:38:39.260
We have a transparency
report we've been

00:38:39.260 --> 00:38:40.490
publishing for over a decade.

00:38:40.490 --> 00:38:42.800
We were the first to start
doing this, and that details

00:38:42.800 --> 00:38:46.040
everything about government
and legal actions,

00:38:46.040 --> 00:38:48.320
logs of your own
admins on your service

00:38:48.320 --> 00:38:50.630
through Cloud Audit
Logging, and now, finally,

00:38:50.630 --> 00:38:54.990
logs of Google admins
through Access Transparency.

00:38:54.990 --> 00:38:57.450
Access Transparency will be
in general availability coming

00:38:57.450 --> 00:38:58.820
very soon.

00:38:58.820 --> 00:39:01.800
It will be available for Compute
Engine, Persistent Disk, Cloud

00:39:01.800 --> 00:39:06.600
Storage, App Engine,
Cloud IAM, and KMS.

00:39:06.600 --> 00:39:09.510
It's available to Platinum
and Gold customers only

00:39:09.510 --> 00:39:11.820
or your equivalent in
Role-Based Support.

00:39:11.820 --> 00:39:14.460
And you can enable it
today on the GCP website.

00:39:14.460 --> 00:39:18.210
Just go to google.com and
search "Access Transparency."

00:39:18.210 --> 00:39:22.020
And it should come
up first, I hope.

00:39:22.020 --> 00:39:23.370
So thanks very much.

00:39:23.370 --> 00:39:26.040
To find out more, you
can head to our website.

00:39:26.040 --> 00:39:28.530
And I'm now going to invite
Liz Vasquez, the head of cloud

00:39:28.530 --> 00:39:31.800
security at HSBC, on stage
to talk a couple of questions

00:39:31.800 --> 00:39:34.530
about how HSBC is using
Access Transparency.

00:39:34.530 --> 00:39:35.492
AUDIENCE: Google that.

00:39:35.492 --> 00:39:37.160
JOSEPH VALENTE: Thanks.

00:39:37.160 --> 00:39:37.660
Sorry?

00:39:37.660 --> 00:39:39.370
AUDIENCE: Google
that and let me know.

00:39:39.370 --> 00:39:40.495
JOSEPH VALENTE: Yeah, sure.

00:39:47.030 --> 00:39:49.080
OK, thanks for joining us, Liz.

00:39:49.080 --> 00:39:50.205
UMESH SHANKAR: Thanks, Liz.

00:39:50.205 --> 00:39:51.440
LIZ VASQUEZ: Hello.

00:39:51.440 --> 00:39:53.731
JOSEPH VALENTE: So Liz, we
heard a little bit yesterday

00:39:53.731 --> 00:39:56.900
in the keynote about how HSBC
is moving a lot of your big data

00:39:56.900 --> 00:39:58.550
stuff and many
other applications

00:39:58.550 --> 00:40:00.110
onto Google Cloud Platform.

00:40:00.110 --> 00:40:02.750
But as a big part of
that, transparency

00:40:02.750 --> 00:40:04.310
was important in
making that move.

00:40:04.310 --> 00:40:06.890
So can you talk to us about
why Access Transparency was

00:40:06.890 --> 00:40:08.510
so important for you?

00:40:08.510 --> 00:40:09.410
LIZ VASQUEZ: Right.

00:40:09.410 --> 00:40:14.610
To that point, so my
job is cloud security.

00:40:14.610 --> 00:40:19.790
So as my company is
moving our data to Google,

00:40:19.790 --> 00:40:21.290
I am the one that
needs to look at,

00:40:21.290 --> 00:40:23.430
what are all the controls
we have in place?

00:40:23.430 --> 00:40:27.080
So we're looking at all the type
of attack vectors, attack paths

00:40:27.080 --> 00:40:29.030
that we have.

00:40:29.030 --> 00:40:31.970
Google is providing us
tons of access controls

00:40:31.970 --> 00:40:36.230
at network layers, access
controls from our admins,

00:40:36.230 --> 00:40:38.480
as you said, all the Cloud
Audit Logging we had.

00:40:38.480 --> 00:40:40.490
But there was a space where--

00:40:40.490 --> 00:40:42.470
that's where the trust comes in.

00:40:42.470 --> 00:40:44.370
And the question kept
coming up where--

00:40:44.370 --> 00:40:47.870
well, what if our data
is accessed by someone

00:40:47.870 --> 00:40:49.740
that's not within our control?

00:40:49.740 --> 00:40:53.780
So HSBC can control
or is looking

00:40:53.780 --> 00:40:56.160
at all of the actions
of our employees, right?

00:40:56.160 --> 00:40:58.130
We are limiting the roles.

00:40:58.130 --> 00:41:00.380
We're limiting what
people can and cannot do,

00:41:00.380 --> 00:41:02.510
what buckets they can access.

00:41:02.510 --> 00:41:05.180
So this was the
last area of trust

00:41:05.180 --> 00:41:08.750
for us-- is, well, what
if our buckets, which

00:41:08.750 --> 00:41:12.650
have this highly private
and restricted data,

00:41:12.650 --> 00:41:14.349
are accessed by Google?

00:41:14.349 --> 00:41:15.890
And then the question
comes up, well,

00:41:15.890 --> 00:41:17.330
don't you trust them, right?

00:41:17.330 --> 00:41:19.760
Oh, this is Google, after all.

00:41:19.760 --> 00:41:21.950
And yes, we do trust them.

00:41:21.950 --> 00:41:24.200
But that's where we always
say, in security, is trust,

00:41:24.200 --> 00:41:25.490
but verify, right?

00:41:25.490 --> 00:41:28.070
So Access Transparency
is really helping

00:41:28.070 --> 00:41:30.420
us to be able to
tell the auditors,

00:41:30.420 --> 00:41:32.530
listen, this is why
we're going to the cloud.

00:41:32.530 --> 00:41:34.400
These are all the
steps we are doing.

00:41:34.400 --> 00:41:36.980
And even when we're
calling Google for support,

00:41:36.980 --> 00:41:40.280
even when it's them legitimately
touching our buckets

00:41:40.280 --> 00:41:41.840
for something that
we've requested,

00:41:41.840 --> 00:41:43.580
we have a way of verifying that.

00:41:43.580 --> 00:41:49.670
So we are alerted when we get
Access Transparency log access

00:41:49.670 --> 00:41:52.970
so that we can say, yeah,
we see that Google accessed

00:41:52.970 --> 00:41:57.167
our bucket, which bucket it
was, and which action was taken.

00:41:57.167 --> 00:41:59.000
So I want to know that
it was a read action.

00:41:59.000 --> 00:42:01.820
I want to know which
bucket they actually read.

00:42:01.820 --> 00:42:04.700
And I want to know that it
was based on a support ticket

00:42:04.700 --> 00:42:05.605
from us.

00:42:05.605 --> 00:42:07.234
So that's really
important for us.

00:42:07.234 --> 00:42:08.150
JOSEPH VALENTE: Great.

00:42:08.150 --> 00:42:09.770
And I think you've already
sort of started touching

00:42:09.770 --> 00:42:10.650
on a little bit of this.

00:42:10.650 --> 00:42:12.200
But I just wanted
to know a bit more

00:42:12.200 --> 00:42:15.200
about how you've got that
alerting system set up

00:42:15.200 --> 00:42:15.860
on your side.

00:42:15.860 --> 00:42:17.240
So you're saying
it's alerts that

00:42:17.240 --> 00:42:18.840
are coming now from your logs.

00:42:18.840 --> 00:42:20.469
LIZ VASQUEZ: So
because, thank god,

00:42:20.469 --> 00:42:22.510
you guys thought about
that and already set it up

00:42:22.510 --> 00:42:24.560
as Stackdriver Logging.

00:42:24.560 --> 00:42:26.350
So we have Stackdriver Logging.

00:42:26.350 --> 00:42:29.030
That's fitting into our seam.

00:42:29.030 --> 00:42:30.957
And I just noticed
yesterday that--

00:42:30.957 --> 00:42:33.290
because we've just started
playing with Security Command

00:42:33.290 --> 00:42:34.400
Center--

00:42:34.400 --> 00:42:36.870
that you're also
bubbling that up there.

00:42:36.870 --> 00:42:40.160
So we also have more plans
of furthering and using

00:42:40.160 --> 00:42:44.520
Security Command Center both
within several of our teams.

00:42:44.520 --> 00:42:47.210
Obviously, the seam
has use cases where

00:42:47.210 --> 00:42:49.540
we can send that to our SOC.

00:42:49.540 --> 00:42:52.280
But yeah, for me, it's just
important-- it's, like, we

00:42:52.280 --> 00:42:53.430
have all the options.

00:42:53.430 --> 00:42:56.715
So we can direct it to our
seam, have use cases there.

00:42:56.715 --> 00:42:59.090
Or if someone actually just
wants to use Security Command

00:42:59.090 --> 00:42:59.880
Center--

00:42:59.880 --> 00:43:02.195
which, we're starting
to figure out

00:43:02.195 --> 00:43:05.510
which people we want to give
access to that as well just

00:43:05.510 --> 00:43:07.790
so that everybody has
visibility-- that it's also

00:43:07.790 --> 00:43:09.140
showing there, which is great.

00:43:09.140 --> 00:43:10.140
JOSEPH VALENTE: Awesome.

00:43:10.140 --> 00:43:12.790
[MUSIC PLAYING]

